## Caveats / known risks

Finding:
The registry is intentionally metadata-only.

Affected surface:
Future Capability Bus / Router / Service branches.

Why it matters:
Passing this branch proves capability metadata contract validation and lookup behavior only. It does not prove action execution, routing, backend selection, cancellation propagation, stream binding, policy enforcement, or graph integration.

Smallest safe correction:
Keep execution work in a separate reviewed bus/router/backend branch.

What still needs verification:
Future execution branches must prove behavior with targeted tests and, when runtime execution surfaces are touched, real-runtime checks as applicable.

---

Finding:
`compatibility.backendKinds` is metadata only.

Affected surface:
Future backend adapter and model bundle work.

Why it matters:
If backend kind metadata starts selecting or configuring backends, this branch would have silently become behavior-bearing.

Smallest safe correction:
Keep backend selection in future router/backend adapter branches. Reject backend options, adapter args, raw payloads, paths, commands, and environment/process fields in this contract.

What still needs verification:
Future backend branches must preserve model identity/path guardrails and define model bundle lookup separately.

---

Finding:
V1 registry rejects duplicate capability names.

Affected surface:
Future versioning/selection semantics.

Why it matters:
This keeps the v1 registry simple and avoids hidden selection rules, but future compatibility work may need parallel versions.

Smallest safe correction:
Add parallel version support only in a future registry-selection branch with explicit selection rules.

What still needs verification:
Future registry/router branches should decide whether version selection belongs in the registry, router, or model/capability service layer.

